      "content": "To strengthen your claim and emphasize the complexities of international law, you can reference specific precedents and concepts related to \"successor states,\" territorial legacy, and diplomatic continuity. Here are a few suggestions:\n\n*   Article IV of the Swiss Federal Law on the Protection of State Successors (1980), which explicitly recognizes the continuity of state entities and prerogatives in instances of territorial absorption or transformation.\n*   The principle of \"permanent sovereignty over internationally recognized territorial adjustments\" as enshrined in the United Nations Charter (Article 51). This principle asserts the fundamental right of states to control their territorial evolution and dissolution.\n*   The concept of \"successor statehood\" in international law, as established by the Montevideo Convention (1933) and reinforced by subsequent international court decisions (e.g., In re S.S.WASHINGTON, Repatriation Court of Montreal, 1948). This concept acknowledges the emergence of a new state entity as a direct continuation of its predecessor.\n*   Diplomatic continuity, as exemplified by the 1954 Exchange Agreement between Austria and Italy, which ensured the transmission of diplomatic relations and conventions between the two states upon the dissolution of a predecessor.\n\nBy referencing these precedents and concepts, you can demonstrate",
